# There is no modem here and no site to call, so what these show
# is their own face: the prompt, the settings, the log.

shot    aterm
cap     ATerm 2.6, a terminal emulator, pointed at a serial device.  The
cap     device has to be an SCF one driven the way its control file in
cap     SYS/ATERM expects; /t1 is not configured here, and it says so.
try     aterm /t1
run     clear
wait    1
run     aterm /t1
wait    10

shot    elm
cap     The full-screen mail reader, opening on the folder that ships for
cap     this account: one message.  `d' deletes, `r' replies, `q' quits.
try     elm
run     elm
wait    10
quit    x

shot    answer
cap     Takes telephone messages as mail, for whoever answers the phone.
cap     `Message to:' wants a name from your own alias file (.elm/aliases
cap     in your home directory, compiled by newalias) -- `self' is one --
cap     then the message, ended by a blank line, which it hands to
cap     `elm -s' to mail.  `bye' leaves.
try     answer
run     newalias
wait    8
run     clear
wait    1
run     answer
wait    6
send    self\r
wait    4
send    Mary called about the disk.\r
wait    2
send    She will ring back at four.\r
wait    2
send    \r
wait    6
send    bye\r
wait    4

shot    arepdaemon
cap     The table lists tester, a reply file, and a mailbox size of 0, so
cap     the letter already in tester's mailbox counts as new.  Started in
cap     the background, it reads the table, answers the letter, writes
cap     what it did to autoreply.log, and records the new mailbox size.
cap     It then sleeps and looks again every hour.
try     arepdaemon &
run     rm -f /dd/USR/LIB/ELM/autoreply.data /dd/USR/LIB/ELM/autoreply.log
wait    2
run     echo "I am away until Monday." > /dd/tmp/away.txt
run     echo "tester /dd/tmp/away.txt 0" > /dd/USR/LIB/ELM/autoreply.data
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/USR/LIB/ELM
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat autoreply.data
wait    3
run     arepdaemon &
wait    8
run     cat autoreply.log
wait    3
run     cat autoreply.data
wait    3
fresh

shot    autoreply
cap     `autoreply' with a file names that file as the reply to send while
cap     you are away; on its own it says whether you are autoreplying and
cap     with what; `off' takes you out of the table.  The arepdaemon card
cap     shows the daemon that sends the replies.
try     autoreply tmp/away.txt
run     rm -f /dd/USR/LIB/ELM/autoreply.data
run     echo "I am away until Monday." > /dd/tmp/away.txt
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     autoreply tmp/away.txt
wait    4
run     autoreply
wait    4
run     autoreply off
wait    4
run     autoreply
wait    4

shot    checkalias
cap     Checks what mail aliases expand to before you rely on them, by asking
cap     elm: `os9' is one of the shared aliases newalias -g compiles, and a
cap     name that is no alias comes back as a UUCP address.  It starts elm
cap     by name, so load elm first.
try     checkalias os9 nosuchalias
run     newalias -g
wait    8
run     load /dd/CMDS/ELM/elm
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     checkalias os9 nosuchalias
wait    6

shot    disable
cap     Turns MTSMon, the monitor that answers logins on a serial port,
cap     off on that port.  It finds the port's descriptor in memory, so
cap     the disk's tty descriptor is loaded first; with it found, it
cap     asks MTSMon and answers that MTSMon isn't running -- MTSMon is
cap     your own OS-9's, and none is started here.
try     disable /tty
run     /h1/CMDS/load /dd/CMDS/DRIVERS/tty
run     disable -?
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     disable /tty
wait    5

shot    enable
cap     The other half of `disable', with an extra option to hold its
cap     prompt until a signal comes in.  Run as `./enable' from its own
cap     directory, CMDS/UUCP -- bash has a builtin of the same name, which
cap     answers first.  Same story as disable: the tty descriptor loaded, it
cap     finds the port and says MTSMon isn't running.
try     ./enable /tty
run     /h1/CMDS/load /dd/CMDS/DRIVERS/tty
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS/UUCP
run     ./enable -?
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     ./enable /tty
wait    5

shot    dotilde
cap     mailx and postnews hand each `~' line typed into a letter to
cap     dotilde, with the letter's file and a few settings after it: the
cap     command, a terminal flag, your user number, the quote character,
cap     the letter, your home directory and the message replied to.  Here
cap     `~r note' appends the file note to the letter.
try     dotilde "~r note" 0 7 ">" letter . x
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/DTL; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/DTL
run     echo "Dear Bob," > /dd/tmp/DTL/letter
run     echo "The disk arrived today." > /dd/tmp/DTL/note
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/DTL
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     dotilde "~r note" 0 7 ">" letter . x
wait    5
run     cat letter
wait    3

shot    fastmail
cap     Sends a file as mail without opening the reader.  -d shows what it
cap     does: it writes the letter's header to a temporary file and runs
cap     `list' on that and the message, piped into smail, which routes and
cap     delivers it.  smail wants this machine's name in SYS/hostname, which
cap     your own system supplies.
try     fastmail -d msg.txt tester
run     load /h1/CMDS/list
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/FMAIL; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/FMAIL; echo "hello from fastmail" > /dd/tmp/FMAIL/msg.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/FMAIL
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     fastmail -d msg.txt tester
wait    6

shot    filter
cap     Its usage, then a message handed to it with no rules file: it says
cap     it could not read .elm/filter_rules in tester's home directory and
cap     mails the message through unsorted.  It wants a scratch device at /r0
cap     (a RAM disk; `mount -r=256k /r0' makes one under os9exec), and it
cap     leaves the terminal without its automatic line feed -- `tmode -lf'
cap     or a fresh login puts that back.
try     filter -v <msg.txt
run     mount -r=256k /r0
wait    2
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC; printf "From tester\rSubject: test\r\rbody\r" > /dd/tmp/EC/msg.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     filter -?
wait    4
run     filter -v <msg.txt
wait    6

shot    frm
cap     Lists who your mail is from, one line each, with the subject:
cap     here the one letter in the folder that ships for this account.
try     frm
run     frm
wait    6

shot    lcasep
cap     Two lines of pathalias output, a host name and a route separated by
cap     a tab: lcasep lower-cases each host name and leaves the route as
cap     it is.
try     lcasep <paths
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/LCP; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/LCP
run     printf "MilkyWay\tMilkyWay!%%s\rPhoenix.UUCP\tMilkyWay!Phoenix!%%s\r" > /dd/tmp/LCP/paths
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/LCP
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat paths
wait    3
run     lcasep <paths
wait    5

shot    listalias
cap     Your own aliases first -- home and self, from .elm/aliases.text --
cap     then the system ones.  sort is loaded first; the listing is sorted
cap     by a pipeline listalias hands to a shell.
try     listalias
run     newalias
wait    8
run     newalias -g
wait    8
run     load /dd/CMDS/sort
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     listalias
wait    6

shot    lmail
cap     Local mail delivery: it reads a message on standard input and adds
cap     it to the named user's mail folder, where Elm, messages and readmsg
cap     find it.  Here a short note goes to your own account, tester, and
cap     arrives at the end of its folder.  A folder lmail makes is readable
cap     by its owner and the super-user only, so mail for someone else is
cap     theirs to read, not yours.
try     lmail tester < tmp/note
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     cat SPOOL/MAIL/tester > tmp/lmail.save; echo 'Subject: hello' > tmp/note; echo '' >> tmp/note; echo 'A note for the tester account.' >> tmp/note
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     lmail tester < tmp/note
wait    6
run     tail -l=5 SPOOL/MAIL/tester
wait    3
snap
run     cat tmp/lmail.save > SPOOL/MAIL/tester
wait    2

shot    mail
size    24 80
cap     The message waiting for tester, shown by its number, sender, date
cap     and first line, and the prompt with the current message's number.  `?' lists its commands; x leaves.  mail wants a scratch
cap     device at /r0 (a RAM disk; `mount -r=256k /r0' makes one under
cap     os9exec).
try     mail
run     mount -r=256k /r0
wait    2
run     mkdir -p /dd/MAIL > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     rm -f /dd/MAIL/tester > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     ksh -c "echo Hello from the collection. | mail tester"
wait    5
run     clear
wait    1
run     mail
wait    7
snap
send    x
wait    2

shot    mailx
cap     One letter rmail delivered: mailx counts it -- one message, one new
cap     -- and Enter shows it.  mailx reads MAIL as a directory holding one
cap     mailbox directory per user, so here MAIL names one.
try     mailx
pages
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MAILX
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MAILX/tester
run     export MAIL=/dd/tmp/MAILX
run     echo 'Subject: hello' > /dd/tmp/MAILX/letter
run     echo '' >> /dd/tmp/MAILX/letter
run     echo 'a line of mail' >> /dd/tmp/MAILX/letter
run     rmail tester < /dd/tmp/MAILX/letter
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MAILX
run     clear
wait    1
run     mailx
wait    6
snap
send    \r
wait    4
snap

shot    makedb
cap     The path aliases smail ships with, in USR/LIB/SMAIL/palias, copied
cap     here as palias.src and built into palias.dir and palias.pag.
try     makedb -o palias palias.src
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC
run     cat /dd/USR/LIB/SMAIL/palias > /dd/tmp/EC/palias.src
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     makedb -o palias palias.src
wait    6
run     ls -l palias.dir palias.pag
wait    5

shot    messages
cap     The count for your mailbox, then with -f for a folder named.
try     messages
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     messages
wait    5
run     messages -f SPOOL/MAIL/tester
wait    5

shot    newalias
cap     Your own alias file compiled, then with -g the system one.
try     newalias
run     clear
wait    1
run     newalias
wait    8
run     newalias -g
wait    8

shot    newmail
cap     Watches for mail arriving and says so; -d also reports the
cap     folder it is watching and its size.
try     newmail -d tester
run     newmail -?
wait    6
run     load /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uuname
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     newmail -d tester
wait    8

shot    nptx
cap     The forms of a full name that mail may be addressed to, each with
cap     the login it belongs to: given the login and the name separated by
cap     a tab, it writes the surname, the initialled forms and the whole.
try     nptx <name.txt
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC; printf "jqpublic\tJohn Q Public\r" > /dd/tmp/EC/name.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     nptx <name.txt
wait    6

shot    pathalias
cap     map says who calls whom, with a cost in brackets: milkyway calls
cap     phoenix, phoenix calls seabass, seabass calls pirsqrd.  pathalias
cap     prints the cheapest route from milkyway to each site; the %s is
cap     where a user name goes.
try     pathalias -l milkyway map
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC
run     printf "milkyway phoenix(20)\rphoenix seabass(30)\rseabass pirsqrd(10)\r" > /dd/tmp/EC/map
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat map
wait    2
run     pathalias -l milkyway map
wait    5

shot    philmail
size    40 80
cap     An off-line mail reader: it checks the mailbox file, then return
cap     shows each letter in turn -- here the one in tester's mailbox --
cap     and `q' quits.
try     philmail
run     clear
wait    1
run     philmail
wait    6
send    \r
wait    5
snap
quit    q\r

shot    printmail
size    40 80
cap     The first message in tester's mailbox, formatted to send to a
cap     printer.  It forks `readmsg' by bare name, so load readmsg first.
try     printmail 1
run     load /dd/CMDS/ELM/readmsg
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     printmail 1
wait    6

shot    pwparse
cap     Parses a password file into the login names the mailer wants,
cap     one per line.
try     pwparse <password
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     pwparse < password
wait    6

shot    readmsg
size    40 80
cap     Message 1 of tester's mailbox, by number.
try     readmsg 1
run     clear
wait    1
run     readmsg 1
wait    6

shot    rmail
cap     A letter delivered to tester: MAIL names a directory holding one
cap     mailbox directory per user, and the letter arrives there as a file
cap     of its own.  (SYS/login sets MAIL to a mailbox file, for elm, so
cap     this card points it at a directory.)
try     rmail tester < letter
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/RMAIL
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/RMAIL/tester
run     export MAIL=/dd/tmp/RMAIL
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/RMAIL
run     echo 'Subject: hello' > letter
run     echo '' >> letter
run     echo 'a line of mail' >> letter
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat letter
wait    2
run     rmail tester < letter
wait    4
run     ls tester
wait    3
run     cat tester/m2*
wait    3

shot    smail
size    24 80
cap     The UUCP mail router.  Given a path file -- made here by pathalias
cap     from a three-hop map -- it turns ralph@pirsqrd into a UUCP route,
cap     phoenix!seabass!pirsqrd!ralph, and with -d it shows its working and
cap     the uux command and letter it would send, without sending them.
cap     -h names this host; a real system names it in SYS/hostname.  The
cap     letter is signed with your login name.
try     smail -h milkyway -p paths -d ralph@pirsqrd <letter
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SMAIL; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SMAIL
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SMAIL
run     printf "milkyway phoenix(20)\rphoenix seabass(30)\rseabass pirsqrd(10)\r" > map
run     pathalias -l milkyway map | sort > paths
run     echo "Subject: hello" > letter
run     clear
run     smail -h milkyway -p paths -d ralph@pirsqrd <letter
wait    6

shot    uupoll
super
cap     Polls a UUCP site for waiting work.  It works in silence -- the
cap     only sign is the poll file it leaves in the site's spool
cap     directory.
try     uupoll seabass
run     ls -l /dd/SPOOL/uucp/seabass
wait    4
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     uupoll seabass
wait    6
run     ls -l SPOOL/uucp/seabass
wait    5

shot    uux
super
size    30 80
cap     Queues `date' to run on uunet, one of the sites in this package's
cap     Systems file (USR/LIB/UUCP).  It writes a command file (C.) and
cap     the job it names into uunet's spool directory; uucico carries them
cap     over on its next call.  The
cap     backslash keeps bash from reading `!' as a history reference.
try     uux uunet\!date
os9     uux "uunet!date"
run     rm -rf /dd/SPOOL/uucp/uunet
run     builtin cd /dd/SPOOL/uucp
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     uux uunet\!date
wait    5
run     ls uunet
wait    3
run     cat uunet/C.*
wait    3

shot    bdecode
cap     batch.txt is what C News's bencode made of a two-line note on the
cap     sending site: the header line, the note in 64 printable
cap     characters, and a trailer carrying the byte count and CRC.
cap     bdecode checks both and writes the note back.
try     bdecode batch.txt
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BDC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BDC
run     printf "Decode the following with bdecode\rSGVsbG8gZnJvbSB0aGUgbmV3cyBzcG9vbC4NQSBzZWNvbmQgbGluZS4N\r/00\r42 baa8\r" > /dd/tmp/BDC/batch.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BDC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat batch.txt
wait    3
run     bdecode batch.txt
wait    5

shot    byteflip
cap     ABCDEFGH read as two four-byte words, each turned end for end:
cap     DCBA and HGFE.  The ten numbers are the word length, where each byte
cap     comes from, the word length again, and where each byte goes.
try     byteflip 4 0 1 2 3 4 3 2 1 0 < in.txt
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BFL > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BFL; printf "ABCDEFGH\r" > /dd/tmp/BFL/in.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BFL
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat in.txt
wait    3
run     byteflip 4 0 1 2 3 4 3 2 1 0 < in.txt > out.txt
wait    5
run     cat out.txt
wait    3

shot    c7decode
cap     `encode' turns a note into the seven-bit form a news batch is
cap     sent in over a link that drops the eighth bit; c7decode turns it
cap     back into the note.
try     c7decode < note.c7
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/C7D; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/C7D; echo "Hello from the news spool." > /dd/tmp/C7D/note.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/C7D
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     encode < note.txt > note.c7
wait    3
run     cat note.c7; echo
wait    3
run     c7decode < note.c7
wait    5

shot    dbz
cap     history holds two lines of the kind C News keeps: a message-id,
cap     the arrival and expiry times, and where the article is filed,
cap     separated by tabs.  dbz builds the .dir and .pag index beside it;
cap     the .pag is sized for a whole news system, not for two lines.
try     dbz history
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/DBZ; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/DBZ
run     printf "<one@site.uucp>\t999999999~-\tcomp.os.os9/1\r<two@site.uucp>\t999999999~-\tcomp.os.os9/2\r" > /dd/tmp/DBZ/history
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/DBZ
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat history
wait    3
run     dbz history
wait    8
run     ls -l
wait    5

shot    expire
super
cap     Deletes news articles past their expiry date.  -t previews
cap     without deleting, walking every newsgroup in the active file.
try     expire -t
run     expire -t
wait    8

shot    newshist
cap     newshist looks message-ids up in the news history, lib/news/history
cap     on /h0, through the index dbz built beside it.  The first id is
cap     there and its line comes back; the second is not.
try     newshist "<one@site.uucp>" "<three@site.uucp>"
run     mkdir -p /dd/lib/news > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf "<one@site.uucp>\t999999999~-\tcomp.os.os9/1\r<two@site.uucp>\t999999999~-\tcomp.os.os9/2\r" > /dd/lib/news/history
run     builtin cd /dd/lib/news
run     dbz history
wait    8
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat history
wait    3
run     newshist "<one@site.uucp>" "<three@site.uucp>"
wait    6

shot    newslock
cap     The news system's lock, given a temporary name and the real lock
cap     name: it links one to the other, so both names show the same file
cap     -- link count 2 in ls -l.  Run again while the lock exists, it
cap     fails, which is what makes it a lock.
# NEVER delete what this card links: os9exec's I$Delete frees a shared
# descriptor on the first delete (reported 2026-09-23), so rm of a linked
# pair corrupts the image.  Shots run on a fresh image, so MAILA is new.
try     newslock tmplock reallock; echo "status $?"
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MAILA > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MAILA
wait    1
run     echo $$ > tmplock
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     newslock tmplock reallock; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     ls -l
wait    3
run     newslock tmplock reallock; echo "status $?"
wait    4

shot    relaynews
cap     C News's relay: it files an article under its newsgroup, numbers
cap     it and records its Message-ID in the history.  Its log line says
cap     what happened -- `+' filed, and fed the same article again, `-'
cap     refused as a duplicate.
try     relaynews -n < article; relaynews -n < article
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/junk /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/control /dd/tmp/CNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local* /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.dir /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.pag > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control 0000000000 00001 y\rjunk 0000000000 00001 y\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/active
run     printf '0\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/artnum
run     printf '' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history
run     builtin cd /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS
run     dbz history
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CNC
run     printf 'Path: somewhere!someone\rFrom: someone@somewhere\rNewsgroups: junk\rSubject: filed by relaynews\rMessage-ID: <1@somewhere>\rDate: 23 Sep 1990 10:00:00 GMT\r\rhello from C News\r' > article
run     printf 'Newsgroups: junk\rSubject: posted with Pnews\r\rread back with rn\r' > post
wait    3
run     clear
run     relaynews -n < article; relaynews -n < article
wait    6

shot    newsrun
fresh
cap     Files the batches waiting in C News's in.coming directory: each is
cap     uncompressed if it needs it, given OS-9 line endings by trlf, handed
cap     to relaynews and removed.  -v reports each name it meets.  This one
cap     came from a Unix site, LF-terminated, and is filed in junk.
try     newsrun -v; cat junk/1
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/junk /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/control /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/bad > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local* /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/1 /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.dir /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.pag > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control 0000000000 00001 y\rjunk 0000000000 00001 y\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/active
run     printf '0\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/artnum
run     printf '' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history
run     builtin cd /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS
run     dbz history
run     builtin cd /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS
run     printf 'Path: far!someone\012From: someone@far\012Newsgroups: junk\012Subject: from a Unix site\012Message-ID: <7@far>\012Date: 23 Sep 1990 10:00:00 GMT\012\012filed by newsrun\012' > in.coming/1
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     load /dd/CMDS/NEWS/trlf
wait    3
run     clear
run     newsrun -v; cat junk/1
wait    8

shot    trlf
cap     Turns LF line endings into OS-9's CR, which is what newsrun needs
cap     of a batch from a Unix site.  A file is converted in place; -s
cap     reads standard input and writes standard output.  The dumps show
cap     the 0A bytes before and the 0D bytes after.
try     dump unix; trlf unix; dump unix
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/TRL > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/TRL
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/TRL
run     printf 'from a\012Unix site\012' > unix
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     dump unix; trlf unix; dump unix
wait    4

shot    Pnews
cap     post is an article with its header written: the group and the
cap     subject, a blank line, the text.  -h hands it to Pnews and -s sends
cap     it without asking; it lands in C News's incoming directory,
cap     SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming on /h0, until relaynews files it.  Bare,
cap     Pnews asks for the distribution, newsgroup and subject itself.
try     Pnews -h post -s
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/junk /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/control /dd/tmp/CNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local* /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.dir /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.pag > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control 0000000000 00001 y\rjunk 0000000000 00001 y\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/active
run     printf '0\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/artnum
run     printf '' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history
run     builtin cd /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS
run     dbz history
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CNC
run     printf 'Path: somewhere!someone\rFrom: someone@somewhere\rNewsgroups: junk\rSubject: filed by relaynews\rMessage-ID: <1@somewhere>\rDate: 23 Sep 1990 10:00:00 GMT\r\rhello from C News\r' > article
run     builtin cd /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS
run     printf 'Newsgroups: junk\rSubject: posted with Pnews\r\rread back with rn\r' > post
wait    3
run     echo yes > /dd/.pnewsexpert
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat post
wait    2
run     Pnews -h post -s
wait    6
run     ls in.coming
wait    3

shot    rn
size    24 80
cap     rn on C News's spool: one article waits in junk, posted with Pnews
cap     and filed by relaynews, and `y' opens it.  Space pages on, n goes to
cap     the next article, q leaves.  rn keeps its scratch files on a RAM
cap     disk, /r0, mounted first.
try     rn junk
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/junk /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/control /dd/tmp/CNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local* /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.dir /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.pag > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control 0000000000 00001 y\rjunk 0000000000 00001 y\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/active
run     printf '0\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/artnum
run     printf '' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history
run     builtin cd /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS
run     dbz history
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CNC
run     printf 'Path: somewhere!someone\rFrom: someone@somewhere\rNewsgroups: junk\rSubject: filed by relaynews\rMessage-ID: <1@somewhere>\rDate: 23 Sep 1990 10:00:00 GMT\r\rhello from C News\r' > article
run     printf 'Newsgroups: junk\rSubject: posted with Pnews\r\rread back with rn\r' > post
wait    3
run     mount -r=256k /r0 > /nil 2>/nil
run     mkdir -p /dd/News > /nil 2>/nil
run     Pnews -h post -s > /nil
run     relaynews -r -n < /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local1
run     printf 'junk:\r' > /dd/.newsrc
wait    3
run     clear
run     rn junk
wait    6
send    y
wait    5
snap
quit    qqq

shot    newsetup
cap     Writes the .newsrc rn reads -- every group in C News's active file,
cap     one to a line, each marked `!' (not subscribed) until you choose --
cap     for someone reading news for the first time.
try     newsetup; cat .newsrc
run     set +H
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/junk /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/control /dd/tmp/CNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/CNEWS/in.coming/0local* /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.dir /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history.pag > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control 0000000000 00001 y\rjunk 0000000000 00001 y\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/active
run     printf '0\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/artnum
run     printf '' > /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS/history
run     builtin cd /h0/USR/LIB/CNEWS
run     dbz history
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/CNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/CNC
run     printf 'Path: somewhere!someone\rFrom: someone@somewhere\rNewsgroups: junk\rSubject: filed by relaynews\rMessage-ID: <1@somewhere>\rDate: 23 Sep 1990 10:00:00 GMT\r\rhello from C News\r' > article
run     printf 'Newsgroups: junk\rSubject: posted with Pnews\r\rread back with rn\r' > post
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd
run     rm -f .newsrc .oldnewsrc > /nil 2>/nil
run     clear
run     newsetup; cat .newsrc
wait    6

shot    setup
size    40 80
fresh
cap     Builds TOP Munich's SysInfo table, a resident module of named
cap     settings and locks, from SYS/sysinfo.  -d shows each line as it is
cap     read: the host name, where MNews keeps its news, and the locks its
cap     programs take.  MNews and nn read it, so run it once first --
cap     once per boot: a second run says `can't create sysinfo', since
cap     the event its first run made is still there.
try     setup -d
run     builtin cd /dd
run     clear
run     setup -d
wait    4

shot    getinfo
fresh
cap     What setup put in the SysInfo table: every entry with -a, its
cap     type and value, and each lock free or held.  Bare it lists only the
cap     locks; -u releases one a program left behind.
try     getinfo -a
run     setup
run     clear
run     getinfo -a
wait    4

shot    inews
super
fresh
cap     A new group made with a control message, then an article posted
cap     into it; -v marks each step's start and end with the time.
try     inews -h -v < article
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     clear
run     inews -c=newgroup:comp.os.os9 -v < /nil
wait    4
run     inews -h -v < article
wait    5

shot    nnmaster
super
size    24 80
fresh
cap     -I sets up nn's database of the articles MNews has stored: it asks
cap     for OK, then whether to reuse the GROUPS file, and here the answers
cap     come from the file ok.  A plain `nnmaster' later collects what has
cap     arrived since.
try     nnmaster -I < ok
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     inews -c=newgroup:comp.os.os9 < /nil
run     inews -h < article
run     printf 'OK\ry\r' > ok
wait    2
run     clear
run     nnmaster -I < ok
wait    6

shot    nnaux
size    24 80
fresh
cap     What nn runs to post, follow up, reply or mail.  nn hands it a work file
cap     holding the article, the line to start an editor on, and what to
cap     do first; `send' posts without asking.  It gives the file to inews
cap     and removes it, and the article is filed in its group.
try     nnaux post article 1 send; cat junk/1
run     setup
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/article /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     builtin cd /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS
run     printf 'Newsgroups: junk\rSubject: posted by nnaux\r\rnn hands nnaux the article to post.\r' > article
wait    2
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     load /dd/CMDS/MNEWS/inews
wait    2
run     clear
run     nnaux post article 1 send
wait    6
run     cat junk/1
wait    2

shot    nn
super
size    24 80
fresh
cap     The nn newsreader on the group MNews filed the article in: a menu
cap     of articles by subject, each with a letter to pick it.  Space reads
cap     on, `?' is help, Q quits.  It reads the database nnmaster keeps.
try     nn comp.os.os9
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     inews -c=newgroup:comp.os.os9 < /nil
run     inews -h < article
run     printf 'OK\ry\r' > ok
run     nnmaster -I < ok > /nil
run     nnmaster < /nil
wait    4
run     clear
run     nn comp.os.os9
wait    8
send    \s
wait    6
snap
quit    Q

shot    nnadmin
super
fresh
cap     nn's database administrator.  Given its commands as an argument
cap     it runs without a menu: C prints the configuration -- where nn's
cap     library, database, news spool and programs are.
try     nnadmin C
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     inews -c=newgroup:comp.os.os9 < /nil
run     inews -h < article
run     printf 'OK\ry\r' > ok
run     nnmaster -I < ok > /nil
run     nnmaster < /nil
wait    4
run     clear
run     nnadmin C
wait    6

shot    encode
cap     B News's encode, which MNews carries: bytes in, printable text
cap     out, for a link that cannot carry eight bits.  decode turns it
cap     back.
try     echo hello world | encode
run     clear
run     echo hello world | encode
wait    4

shot    decode
cap     The other half of encode: the printable text back into the bytes
cap     it came from.
try     echo hello world | encode | decode
run     clear
run     echo hello world | encode | decode
wait    4

shot    cvt_help
cap     Converts nn's help files: each `;:' pair and the letter after it
cap     become one control byte, which nn reads as a highlight.  xd
cap     shows the bytes it made -- 01 either side of the word.
try     echo ';:Ahello;:A' | cvt_help | xd
run     clear
run     echo ';:Ahello;:A' | cvt_help | xd
wait    4

shot    sbatch
fresh
cap     Collects the articles waiting for a neighbouring site and passes
cap     them on as a batch.  Here a neighbour `nbr' has been added to the
cap     Sys file for the purpose and one article posted; sbatch spools it
cap     and hands the batch to uux -- which answers that UUCP has no host
cap     called nbr, as it would until one is set up.
try     sbatch nbr
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/BATCH/nbr > /nil 2>/nil
run     cp /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Sys sys.save
run     echo 'nbr:.*:*' >> /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Sys
run     load /dd/CMDS/UUCP/uux
run     inews -h < article
wait    3
run     clear
run     sbatch nbr
wait    8
snap
run     cp sys.save /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Sys

shot    tass
size    24 80
cap     tass on an MNews "pre 2" spool made for the card: two articles in
cap     comp.os.os9, found through SysInfo's MNEWS.DIR and MNEWS.LIB.  Return
cap     enters a group and reads an article; q goes back.
try     tass
run     set +H
run     W=/dd/tmp/TASSC; rm -rf $W > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p $W/lib $W/spool/comp/os/os9
run     printf 'MNEWS.DIR,s=%s/spool\rMNEWS.LIB,s=%s/lib\r' $W $W > $W/sysinfo; setup $W/sysinfo
run     printf 'comp.os.os9 00000002 00000001 nu\r' > $W/lib/Active
run     printf 'Path: osk\rFrom: a@osk (A Writer)\rNewsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: first article\rMessage-ID: <1@osk>\rDate: 1 Jan 93 10:00:00 GMT\rLines: 1\r\rfirst body\r' > $W/spool/comp/os/os9/1
run     printf 'Path: osk\rFrom: b@osk (B Writer)\rNewsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: second article\rMessage-ID: <2@osk>\rDate: 2 Jan 93 10:00:00 GMT\rLines: 1\r\rgreetings, reader\r' > $W/spool/comp/os/os9/2
run     rm -f $HOME/.newsrc > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     tass
wait    5
send    \r
wait    3
snap
send    q
wait    2
send    q
wait    2

shot    nncheck
super
fresh
cap     Says whether there is news you have not read.  nn has to have been
cap     run once to set up your own files first; after that nncheck counts
cap     what is waiting.
try     nncheck
run     setup
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MNC > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/comp > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/control > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/MNEWS/junk > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/GROUPS > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /h0/SPOOL/NN/MASTER > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -f /h0/SPOOL/NN/DATA/*.? /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/HISTORY/* > /nil 2>/nil
run     printf 'control:00000 00000 n\rjunk:00000 00000 n\r' > /h0/USR/LIB/NEWS/Active
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MNC
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MNC
run     printf 'Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: MNews on this disk\r\rPosted with inews, read back with nn.\r' > article
wait    2
run     load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     inews -c=newgroup:comp.os.os9 < /nil
run     inews -h < article
run     printf 'OK\ry\r' > ok
run     nnmaster -I < ok > /nil
run     nnmaster < /nil
wait    4
run     nn comp.os.os9
wait    8
send    Q
wait    4
send    \r
wait    3
run     clear
run     nncheck
wait    5

shot    postnews
size    44 80
cap     postnews asks for the newsgroup, subject, keywords, distribution and
cap     summary, then reads the body up to a line with just a dot.  It
cap     builds the header and hands the article to rnews, which files it in
cap     the news spool.  It starts rnews by bare name, so load UUCP's rnews
cap     first.
try     postnews
pages
run     load /dd/CMDS/UUCP/rnews
run     rm -rf /dd/SPOOL/news/COMP > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     postnews
wait    6
send    comp.os.os9\r
wait    3
send    a test posting\r
wait    3
send    \r
wait    3
send    world\r
wait    3
send    testing postnews\r
wait    4
send    This is only a test.\r
wait    3
snap
send    .\r
wait    10
run     ls -R SPOOL/news/COMP
wait    4
snap

shot    readnews
size    30 80
cap     Reads Usenet news articles.  With nothing in .newsrc matching
cap     most of the active groups, it asks about each one in turn before
cap     it would start showing articles.
try     readnews
run     readnews
wait    6
send    n\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    3
send    n\r
wait    4

shot    rnews
super
size    30 80
cap     batch.txt is one article behind a `#! rnews <length>' line, the
cap     length being the article's size in bytes.  rnews files it under the
cap     directory tree its `Newsgroups:' line names, in the news spool.
try     rnews batch.txt
run     rm -rf /dd/SPOOL/news/COMP /dd/art.txt /dd/batch.txt
wait    3
run     printf "Newsgroups: comp.os.os9\rSubject: test article\r\rhello there\r" > /dd/art.txt
wait    3
run     LEN=$(cat /dd/art.txt | wc -c)
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd
run     printf "#! rnews %s\r" $LEN > batch.txt; cat art.txt >> batch.txt
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat batch.txt
wait    3
run     rnews batch.txt
wait    6
run     ls -laR SPOOL/news/COMP
wait    6

shot    subscribe
cap     .newsrc in your home directory lists the groups you read, with
cap     `:' after a group you are subscribed to and `!' after one you have
cap     turned off.  subscribe turns news.announce.newusers back on.  A
cap     group that is not in .newsrc at all is left alone.
try     subscribe news.announce.newusers
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SUBS; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SUBS
run     echo "comp.os.os9: 1-12" > /dd/tmp/SUBS/.newsrc
run     echo "news.announce.newusers! 1-3" >> /dd/tmp/SUBS/.newsrc
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/SUBS
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SUBS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat .newsrc
wait    3
run     subscribe news.announce.newusers
wait    5
run     cat .newsrc
wait    4
snap
run     export HOME=/dd
wait    1

shot    unsubscribe
cap     .newsrc in your home directory lists the groups you read, with
cap     `:' after a group you are subscribed to and `!' after one you have
cap     turned off.  unsubscribe turns comp.sys.m68k off and keeps its
cap     record of what was read.
try     unsubscribe comp.sys.m68k
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SUBS; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SUBS
run     echo "comp.os.os9: 1-12" > /dd/tmp/SUBS/.newsrc
run     echo "comp.sys.m68k: 1-40" >> /dd/tmp/SUBS/.newsrc
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/SUBS
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SUBS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat .newsrc
wait    3
run     unsubscribe comp.sys.m68k
wait    5
run     cat .newsrc
wait    4
snap
run     export HOME=/dd
wait    1

# Communications, everything but Mail and News.  Almost all of these talk
# to a peer that is not here -- a serial line, a modem, a remote UUCP or
# TCP host, a Kermit partner, a web client.  The honest card is what each
# says when it reaches for that peer, or what it does on its own.

# --- Terminal & session ---------------------------------------------------

shot    cls
cap     Clears the screen through the terminal's termcap clear capability,
cap     the way `clear' beside it does, and returns with the cursor at the
cap     top.  A directory listing is printed and then wiped -- only the
cap     line typed after it is left.
try     dir CMDS; cls; echo "cls wiped the listing above"
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     dir CMDS
wait    3
run     cls
wait    2
run     echo "cls wiped the listing above"
wait    3
snap

shot    fkeys
size    32 80
cap     Programs a VT220's function keys from a definition file -- `6=dir\n'
cap     makes F6 type `dir' and press return -- and the disk ships one in
cap     DOC/fkeys.  What it sends is a DECUDK control string, which a
cap     terminal that is not a VT220 will not show, so it is put through
cap     `dump' here: `ESC P 0;1|' opens it, then each key as a number and
cap     its text in hex.  It names each key as it goes.
try     fkeys f_keys > fk; dump fk
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/FKY; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/FKY
run     cp /dd/DOC/fkeys/f_keys /dd/tmp/FKY/f_keys
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/FKY
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat f_keys
wait    3
run     fkeys f_keys > fk; dump fk
wait    5

shot    sbreak
cap     Sends a break on a serial (SCF) device named on its command line --
cap     `sbreak /t1'.  It needs a serial device to act on; with none named
cap     it says so.
try     sbreak
run     clear
wait    1
run     sbreak
wait    4

shot    initvdu
cap     -d shows what it would send to initialise the terminal from its
cap     termcap entry, without sending it.
try     initvdu -d
run     clear
wait    1
run     initvdu -d
wait    5

shot    setterm
cap     Reports or sets the terminal type.  Run with no argument it prints
cap     what TERM currently names; give it a name to change it.
try     setterm
run     setterm
wait    5

shot    setfont
cap     Loads a downloadable font into a terminal from a font file.  It
cap     needs real terminal hardware to receive the font; run without a
cap     path it prints its one-line usage.
try     setfont
run     setfont
wait    4

shot    tsmon2
cap     A terminal monitor: it watches a serial device and starts the login
cap     program when someone types RETURN on it, or with -i as soon as
cap     carrier appears.  Pointed at /t1, which is not configured here, it
cap     cannot open the device.
try     tsmon2 /t1
run     clear
wait    1
run     tsmon2 /t1
wait    10

shot    connect
cap     Joins two serial paths so you can talk through one to the other,
cap     with switches for echo, line-ending translation and flow control
cap     on each side.  With no paths named both ends are the terminal
cap     itself: it reports the connection, names the terminal's quit
cap     character, and waits.
try     connect
run     connect
wait    5
snap

shot    tsu
cap     tterm's setup program: it checks each directory and file tterm needs
cap     under USR and creates any that is missing -- on a fresh disk, the
cap     TTERM directory and your dial file.
try     tsu
run     tsu
wait    6

shot    input
cap     Three lines through unchanged, a character at a time.
try     input <lines.txt
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC; printf "the first line\rthe second line\rand the third\r" > /dd/tmp/EC/lines.txt
wait    3
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     input <lines.txt
wait    5

shot    wysecrack
cap     Wisecracks for a Wyse terminal: a line a minute, twelve of them,
cap     sent after ESC F so that a Wyse shows each in its status line.
cap     This terminal is not a Wyse, so they land in the text, the first
cap     a minute in, each behind the F of the ESC F it does not know.
try     wysecrack
run     clear
run     wysecrack
wait    126
kill

# --- Terminal & transfer --------------------------------------------------

shot    wysetime
cap     A Wyse terminal clock-setter in BASIC09: it writes the date and
cap     time, then the escape sequence a Wyse smart terminal reads to set
cap     its own on-screen clock.  The bytes are shown through `dump', being
cap     terminal control codes rather than text.
try     runb wysetime > wt.out; dump wt.out
run     load /h1/CMDS/runb
wait    2
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/WYT; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/WYT
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/WYT
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     runb wysetime > wt.out; dump wt.out
wait    5

shot    sterm
cap     A serial terminal emulator with a built-in B+ file transfer.  It
cap     links to the port MODEM names, or -l gives, and with neither it
cap     says so and stops.
try     sterm
run     sterm; echo ""
wait    5

shot    tterm
cap     A VT100-style terminal emulator.  It links to the port MODEM names,
cap     or -l gives, and with neither it says so and stops.  `tsu' makes
cap     the directories it keeps its dialling files in.
try     tterm
run     tterm
wait    5

shot    xy
cap     xy waiting to receive a file by XMODEM or YMODEM.  With no MODEM
cap     port set it talks on the terminal itself, so the `C' it sends to
cap     ask the far end for a CRC transfer lands on the screen; a sender
cap     on the line would answer with the first block.  Name files to
cap     send instead.
try     xy
run     xy
wait    6
snap

shot    z
cap     z waiting to receive by ZMODEM.  With no MODEM port set it talks on
cap     the terminal, so its receive-ready frame, sent again every few
cap     seconds until the far end answers, is what shows.  Name files to
cap     send instead; -p names a port.
try     z
run     z
wait    8
snap

shot    k
cap     k waiting for the first Kermit packet on the terminal, since no
cap     MODEM port is set; a sender on the line would start the transfer.
cap     Name files to send instead.  The same family as `xy' and `z'.
try     k
run     k
wait    5
snap

shot    dld
cap     dld sending a file down the line by XMODEM -- in its vocabulary a
cap     download goes from this machine to the terminal.  It announces the
cap     transfer and waits for the receiver's first request.
try     dld motd
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     dld motd
wait    5
snap

shot    uld
cap     uld receiving a file by XMODEM into the name given -- an upload,
cap     from the terminal to this machine, the companion to `dld'.  It
cap     creates the file, announces the transfer and sends the `C' that
cap     asks the far end for a CRC transfer.
try     uld got.txt
run     rm -f /dd/tmp/got.txt
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     uld got.txt
wait    5
snap

shot    txmod
cap     Sends OS-9 modules over a serial line to rxmod on another machine,
cap     which enters them in that machine's module directory.  It needs
cap     that line and rxmod at the far end; run on its own it prints its
cap     syntax.
try     txmod
run     clear
wait    1
run     txmod
wait    5

shot    rxmod
cap     Receives OS-9 modules over a serial line from txmod on another
cap     machine and enters them in the module directory.  It needs that
cap     line, a host running txmod, and the vmod_trap handler that ships
cap     beside it, loaded first.  Asked for help it prints its syntax,
cap     under a line of symbols of its own.
try     rxmod -?
run     load /dd/CMDS/COMMS/vmod_trap
wait    3
run     clear
wait    1
run     rxmod -?
wait    5

shot    blastem
cap     blastem asked to send a file by XMODEM: it opens the modem path,
cap     /t0 unless -p names another, and there is no such device on this
cap     system, so it says so and stops.  -y is YMODEM, -r receive.
try     blastem -s motd
run     builtin cd /dd/SYS
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     blastem -s motd; echo ""
wait    5

shot    xydown
cap     An XMODEM/YMODEM/YModem-Batch download that senses which the sender
cap     is using and follows.  Run bare it opens the serial port, /T0
cap     unless -t names another, and stops when there is no such device.
try     xydown
run     xydown
wait    5

shot    xyt
cap     An X/Y/YModem-Batch transfer for the `tterm' emulator, here in
cap     remote mode (-x), where it talks on its standard input: ready to
cap     receive, it sends the `C' that asks the far end for a CRC transfer,
cap     and a sender on the line would answer with the first block.  Run
cap     locally it uses the MODEM port, or the one -t names.  It logs to
cap     SYS/XYTRANSFER, which `tsu' makes; without it it says so and goes on.
try     xyt -x
run     clear
wait    1
run     xyt -x
wait    8
snap

# --- Kermit ---------------------------------------------------------------

shot    kermit
cap     OS-9 Kermit 1.5, a serial file-transfer and terminal program.  It
cap     needs a serial line to a Kermit at the other end; with none named
cap     it prints its command letters.
try     kermit
run     clear
wait    1
run     kermit
wait    6

shot    kermit2
cap     Kermit 1.6, the same command letters as `kermit' in less space.
cap     It prints them when run with no line to transfer over.
try     kermit2
run     kermit2
wait    6

shot    kermit3
cap     Kermit68K 1.0, a different design: it puts up its own `Kermit68K>'
cap     prompt and reads commands and a Kermit.ini, rather than taking
cap     command letters.
try     kermit3
run     kermit3
wait    6
snap
send    exit\r
wait    2

shot    xkermit
cap     OS-9 Kermit 1.5 again -- the same version as `kermit' in half the
cap     space, because it links the shared cio library rather than carrying
cap     its own stdio.  It needs a serial line to a Kermit at the other end;
cap     with none named it prints its command letters.
try     xkermit
run     clear
wait    1
run     xkermit
wait    5

shot    ckermit
size    34 80
cap     C-Kermit 5A(190), the full protocol with a command language, at
cap     its `C-Kermit>' prompt.  `show features' lists what this build
cap     carries: character-set translation, a fullscreen transfer display.
try     ckermit
run     clear
wait    1
run     ckermit
wait    8
send    show features\r
wait    4
snap
send    exit\r
wait    2

shot    finger
cap     tester has no full name in the password file, no .project and no
cap     .plan, so finger shows the home directory and the shell and says
cap     so for the rest.
try     finger tester
run     clear
wait    1
run     finger tester
wait    8

shot    bm
size    30 80
cap     bm, the mailer that goes with net, writing a letter: it adds the
cap     headers and queues it -- the letter, and beside it the envelope
cap     net's SMTP reads (host, sender, recipient) -- for net to send.  Its
cap     setup is a .bmrc in NETHOME and the spool in NETSPOOL.
try     echo "hello from bm" | bm -s "a test" someone@far.ampr
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BMK > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BMK/home /dd/tmp/BMK/spool/mail /dd/tmp/BMK/spool/mqueue /dd/tmp/BMK/spool/rqueue
run     echo "host test.ampr" > /dd/tmp/BMK/home/.bmrc; echo "user tester" >> /dd/tmp/BMK/home/.bmrc; echo "fullname Test_User" >> /dd/tmp/BMK/home/.bmrc
run     export NETHOME=/dd/tmp/BMK/home NETSPOOL=/dd/tmp/BMK/spool
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BMK/spool
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     echo "hello from bm" | bm -s "a test" someone@far.ampr
wait    3
run     cat mqueue/1.txt
wait    2
run     cat mqueue/1.wrk
wait    2

shot    net
size    30 80
cap     The KA9Q net -- TCP/IP over SLIP or AX.25, with telnet,
cap     ftp and smtp.  It starts, then lists the files it needs you to
cap     write for your own station: startup.net, the host and user tables,
cap     the mail directories.  Its setup guide is Chapter 2 of DOC/net.
try     net
run     net
wait    8

shot    osknet
size    36 80
cap     OSKNET, a TCP/IP package for OS-9 built on KA9Q --
cap     telnet, ftp, ping and smtp over SLIP or PPP.  It starts and lists
cap     the configuration it needs for your own station: startup.okn, the
cap     host and user tables, the mail directories.  Setup is in DOC/osknet.
try     osknet
run     clear
wait    1
run     osknet
wait    8
snap

shot    msntp
cap     Sets the system clock from a network time server: name the server
cap     and it asks one, then sets the clock.  Named one here it stops at
cap     the lookup -- there is no host table on this disk to find an
cap     address in, and it says so for a dotted number as readily as for a
cap     name.  With no server named it listens for broadcasts instead and
cap     waits for one, which is what its own manual says it does.
try     msntp 127.0.0.1
run     clear
wait    1
run     msntp 127.0.0.1
wait    6

shot    boa
cap     Boa started in the background on port 8080, one request sent to it
cap     with ttcp (which carries what it reads to a port), and its access
cap     log showing the answer -- 200 and the size of the page.  get.req
cap     holds the request, `GET /index.html HTTP/1.0' and a blank line,
cap     each ending in CR LF.  A browser pointed at the port gets the same
cap     page.
try     boa &
run     rm -f /dd/c/unid/boa/osk/logs/access_log /dd/c/unid/boa/osk/logs/error_log
run     echo "GET /index.html HTTP/1.0" > /dd/tmp/boa.cr; echo "" >> /dd/tmp/boa.cr
run     autolf -c -C -L < /dd/tmp/boa.cr > /dd/c/unid/boa/osk/get.req
wait    2
run     builtin cd /h0/c/unid/boa/osk
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     boa &
wait    6
run     ttcp -t -p8080 127.0.0.1 < get.req > /nil
wait    8
run     cat logs/access_log
wait    3
fresh

shot    ttcp
cap     Times a transfer between two machines -- here one machine and
cap     itself: `ttcp -r -s' waits in the background, `ttcp -t -s' sends it
cap     a megabyte over TCP, and each side reports the rate.  Name the
cap     other machine's address to measure a real link.
try     ttcp -r -s & ttcp -t -s 127.0.0.1
run     clear
run     ttcp -r -s & ttcp -t -s 127.0.0.1
wait    8

shot    nslookup
cap     Looks a host name up in the domain name system.  It reads the name
cap     server's address from resolv.conf at the root of /h0 -- the example
cap     in DOC/bind, copied there, names 127.0.0.1 -- and asks it; nothing
cap     answers there, so it says the server is not available.  Name a real
cap     name server in resolv.conf to get addresses back.
try     nslookup ftp.funet.fi
run     cat /dd/DOC/bind/resolv.conf > /h0/resolv.conf
run     clear
run     nslookup ftp.funet.fi
wait    4
snap
run     rm /h0/resolv.conf

shot    nsquery
cap     BIND's small resolver test: given a host, it prints the names and
cap     addresses the name server returns.  With no name server to reach
cap     it says the lookup failed.
try     nsquery ftp.funet.fi
run     nsquery ftp.funet.fi
wait    4

shot    atp
size    34 80
cap     ATP's banner and its Reader prompt, with no QWK packet loaded yet.
cap     It reads its configuration first -- atprc or .atprc in your home
cap     directory, or in the directory ATP names; a working one for this
cap     disk ships in DOC/ka9q, and this card uses a copy of it.
try     atp
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/ATPK > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/ATPK; cp /dd/DOC/ka9q/atprc /dd/tmp/ATPK
run     export ATP=/dd/tmp/ATPK
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     atp
wait    10

shot    infoxpress
cap     An InfoXpress network client.  It opens a serial device to reach
cap     its host and waits there for the far end, which no line here
cap     provides.
try     infoxpress
run     infoxpress
wait    8

# --- UUCP -----------------------------------------------------------------

shot    uucp
size    30 80
cap     The UUCP copy command, queueing a note to go to phoenix, one of
cap     the sites in SYS/UUCP/Systems.  It writes a command file (C.) and
cap     a copy of the data (D.) into phoenix's spool directory, where
cap     uucico finds them on its next call.  The site's spool directory
cap     has to exist.  The backslash keeps bash from reading `!' as a
cap     history reference.
try     uucp tmp/UUCPQ/note.txt phoenix\!/tmp/note.txt
os9     uucp tmp/UUCPQ/note.txt "phoenix!/tmp/note.txt"
run     rm -rf /dd/SPOOL/uucp/phoenix /dd/tmp/UUCPQ
run     mkdir -p /dd/SPOOL/uucp/phoenix
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/UUCPQ
run     echo "a note for phoenix" > /dd/tmp/UUCPQ/note.txt
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     uucp tmp/UUCPQ/note.txt phoenix\!/tmp/note.txt
wait    5
run     ls SPOOL/uucp/phoenix
wait    3
run     cat SPOOL/uucp/phoenix/C.*
wait    3

shot    uucico
cap     The UUCP transfer program itself.  Given a site name it dials that
cap     site and moves the queued files; -r answers as the slave instead.
cap     Run with neither it says what it is missing.
try     uucico
run     uucico
wait    5

shot    uuxqt
cap     Runs the commands a remote site queued here.  Before starting it
cap     looks for a module called `procs' to see whether another copy is
cap     already going -- your own OS-9's, loaded from /h1 here.  With it
cap     resident it gets past that and asks which site's queue to run,
cap     printing what it takes: a system name, or `ALL' for every system in
cap     the Systems file.
try     uuxqt
run     load /h1/CMDS/procs
wait    4
run     clear
wait    1
run     uuxqt
wait    6

shot    uuname
cap     The UUCP sites this system is configured to reach, out of the
cap     tables in SYS -- and `-l' for the name this machine answers to,
cap     which is what the other end sees.
try     uuname; uuname -l
run     clear
wait    1
run     uuname
wait    5
run     uuname -l
wait    5

shot    uulog
size    24 108
cap     Shows the UUCP transfer log, an event a line.  The log ships empty,
cap     so the card makes an entry to read back: `rmail' attempts a local
cap     delivery, does not find the mailbox in the shape it wants, and logs
cap     the failure -- and uulog prints it, with the site, the time and the
cap     reason.  `-s' narrows a real log to one named site.
try     rmail tester; uulog
# The log is APPENDED to, so without this the capture grows a line every
# time the sheet is re-shot against an image that was not rebuilt -- and a
# card whose screen changes for no reason of the program's is a card that
# will read as drift later.  One entry is what a reader gets on a fresh
# disk, so one entry is what the card shows.
run     rm -f /dd/LOG/uulog
run     clear
wait    2
run     rmail tester; uulog
wait    6

shot    uuclean
super
cap     Removes stale jobs from the UUCP spool.  Its help lists the age
cap     and selection options.
try     uuclean -?
run     uuclean -?
wait    6

# --- File transfer --------------------------------------------------------

shot    fileserv
size    44 80
cap     The request is an ordinary letter whose body is fileserv's commands:
cap     reply to tester, list the directory, stop.  fileserv answers by
cap     mail, through rmail, and the answer to `dir' is the list of files
cap     it offers.  The server must be switched on in SYS/UUCP/Parameters
cap     (`server = yes'); rmail wants MAIL to be a directory, one mailbox
cap     directory per user.
try     fileserv <tmp/fsreq; ls tmp/FSMAIL/tester
run     builtin cd /dd
run     rm -f /dd/LOG/FileServ > /nil 2>/nil
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/FSMAIL
run     mkdir -p /dd/tmp/FSMAIL/tester
run     export MAIL=/dd/tmp/FSMAIL
run     cp /dd/SYS/UUCP/Parameters /dd/tmp/Parameters.fs
run     grep -v "^server = no" /dd/tmp/Parameters.fs > /dd/SYS/UUCP/Parameters
run     echo "From tester Mon Sep 21 00:00:00 2026" > /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     echo "Subject: request" >> /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     echo "" >> /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     echo "reply tester" >> /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     echo "dir" >> /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     echo "quit" >> /dd/tmp/fsreq
run     load /dd/CMDS/UUCP/rmail
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat tmp/fsreq
wait    2
run     fileserv <tmp/fsreq; ls tmp/FSMAIL/tester
wait    6
run     cat tmp/FSMAIL/tester/m2*
wait    3
snap
# Put the shipped Parameters back, and the log: it ships absent, and
# `driven.cases' asserts that uulog cannot open it.
run     cp /dd/tmp/Parameters.fs /dd/SYS/UUCP/Parameters
run     rm -f /dd/LOG/FileServ
wait    1

shot    fixtext
cap     Repairs the line endings of a text batch received over UUCP.  ft.in
cap     has Unix line ends, LF (0A); fixtext rewrites them, and the second
cap     dump shows each line now ended by CR (0D), as OS-9 text wants.
try     fixtext ft.in ft.out
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/EC; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/EC
run     printf "line one\012line two\012" > /dd/tmp/EC/ft.in
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/EC
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
wait    1
run     dump ft.in
wait    3
run     fixtext ft.in ft.out
wait    4
run     dump ft.out
wait    4

shot    wn
size    40 80
cap     WN serving a page.  It is an inetd-style server: one HTTP request on
cap     standard input, one response on standard output.  rq holds the
cap     request line and a blank line; autolf ends each with the carriage
cap     return and line feed HTTP wants, and turns the reply's back into
cap     OS-9 line ends for the screen.  Back comes HTTP/1.0 200 with
cap     Last-modified, Content-length and the page, cut here to eighteen
cap     lines.  Its document root is on this disk under /h0, so mount the
cap     collection as /h0 as well as /dd; `wndex' builds the cache it
cap     serves from.
try     wn -L /nil -l /nil <rq.crlf | autolf -N | head -n 18
os9     wn -L /nil -l /nil <rq.crlf ! autolf -N ! head -n 18
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/WNQ; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/WNQ
run     builtin cd /h0/c/unid/wn_1.14.3/osk
run     wndex 2>/nil
wait    6
run     echo "GET /index.html HTTP/1.0" > /dd/tmp/WNQ/rq
wait    2
run     echo "" >> /dd/tmp/WNQ/rq
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/WNQ
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat rq
wait    2
run     autolf -c -C -L < rq > rq.crlf
wait    3
run     wn -L /nil -l /nil <rq.crlf | autolf -N | head -n 18
wait    8

shot    wndex
cap     wndex works on the current directory, so the reader changes to the
cap     directory first -- here WN's document root.  The listing after it
cap     is the index.cache it wrote.
try     wndex; ls -l index.cache
run     builtin cd /dd/c/unid/wn_1.14.3/osk
wait    1
run     rm -f index.cache > /nil 2>/nil
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     wndex; ls -l index.cache
wait    8

shot    inetd
cap     The internet superserver: it listens on a port and hands each
cap     connection to WN.  To listen it needs a TCP/IP stack presenting
cap     the /socket device; run with no arguments it prints its usage.
try     inetd
run     clear
wait    1
run     inetd
wait    5

shot    inetdc
cap     What inetd forks to handle each connection.  It carries no message
cap     strings and, run by hand with nothing to serve, exits in silence;
cap     there is nothing else it can do alone.
try     inetdc
run     inetdc < /nil
wait    5

shot    authwn
cap     WN's authenticator for a password-protected directory: wn runs it
cap     per request, naming the password file and handing it the
cap     request's `Basic user:password' line on standard input.  It
cap     answers only in its exit status -- 0 lets the request in, 1 turns
cap     it away.  The file holds `user:password' lines.
try     echo "Basic tester:secret" | authwn pw; echo "status $?"
os9     echo Basic tester:secret ! authwn pw
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/AWN; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/AWN
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/AWN
run     echo "tester:secret" > pw
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     cat pw
wait    2
run     echo "Basic tester:secret" | authwn pw; echo "status $?"
wait    4
run     echo "Basic tester:guess" | authwn pw; echo "status $?"
wait    4

# --- Preserved here, catalogued under System & modules --------------------

shot    screen
cap     A login greeting: it runs a file picked at random from
cap     $HOME/.screens -- here the only one there, which shows the
cap     message of the day.
try     screen
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/SCRN; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/SCRN/.screens
run     echo "cat /dd/SYS/motd" > /dd/tmp/SCRN/.screens/hi
run     /h1/CMDS/load /h1/CMDS/shell
run     export HOME=/dd/tmp/SCRN
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/SCRN
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     screen
wait    5
snap
run     export HOME=/dd
wait    1

shot    vmod_trap
size    28 80
cap     The trap handler that rxmod and txmod call -- a trap-handler
cap     module, not a program.  modinfo shows what it is, including the
cap     supervisor-state attribute that decides how it behaves once
cap     installed.  Asking a shell to run it can only produce a refusal.
try     modinfo vmod_trap | head -n 14
os9     modinfo vmod_trap ! head -n 14
run     builtin cd /dd/CMDS/COMMS
wait    1
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     modinfo vmod_trap | head -n 14
wait    8
snap

shot    tput
size    16 80
cap     Asks termcap what this terminal needs for a capability, by its
cap     System V name.  `cols' answers with a number; `clear' answers
cap     with the escape sequence itself, shown here through xd so that the
cap     bytes can be read.
try     tput -Tvt100 cols; tput -Tvt100 clear | xd -c
os9     tput -Tvt100 cols; tput -Tvt100 clear ! xd -c
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     tput -Tvt100 cols
wait    3
run     tput -Tvt100 clear | xd -c
wait    4

shot    vttest
size    24 80
cap     vttest's menu: each test draws pages that say what a correct VT100
cap     shows.  It judges the terminal it runs on, so a screen that is not a
cap     full VT100 shows its differences.
try     vttest
run     vttest
wait    6
snap
send    0\r
wait    4

shot    uustat
cap     It answers that uucp is possibly active and reports nothing
cap     further, with a job queued for uunet or without.  Its options,
cap     listed by -?, show the queue by system or by user and kill or
cap     rejuvenate a job.
try     uustat
run     clear
wait    1
run     uustat
wait    4
